From 0f27ef552a32cf78cc376a7edd0272f342cf1c7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zhaosiying12138 <neucszhaopeng2016@163.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 07:18:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 37/42] vega: give SDWA a shared operand layer instead of 225
 fail-closed panics

SDWA is an encoding-level modifier that selects a byte or word sub-field of a
source operand and merges the result into a sub-field of the destination. It is
orthogonal to the opcode, but gem5's Vega ISA had no shared read-operand path
for it: every opcode's execute() constructed its own operands, so there was
nowhere for the modifier to live. Upstream's only consistent option was to fail
closed, and it stamped panic_if(isSDWAInst(), "SDWA not implemented") into 225
hand-expanded bodies across VOP1, VOP2 and VOPC. Three different opcodes killed
three different model runs, and a per-opcode fix could never have converged.

Introduce the encoding-level helpers the ISA actually needs -- the opcode
supplies a pure lane lambda and the shared layer applies source selection, sign
extension, float modifiers and destination merge -- and route the hand-expanded
bodies through them.

A second, silent defect sat behind the panic. Under SDWA the real SRC0 selector
lives in the second dword, so instData.SRC0 holds only the 0xf9 marker. Any fix
that corrected the arithmetic alone would still have registered register 249 in
srcOps, leaving the true source VGPR out of the virtual-to-physical map and out
of RAW hazard tracking. initOperandInfo now registers the second-dword operands.

UNVERIFIED AT HANDOFF: this body of work is carried forward from earlier
sessions. It is compiled into the current binary and both lanes run against it,
but no completed model run has accepted it.

